// src/kernel/core/driver/blob_load.pdx — R29.M4-003 (#1034)
//
// Blob-driver DUAL-SIGNATURE enforcement (blob-policy D1.a).
//
// Policy: design/drivers/blob-policy.md §1.1 (the two-key requirement),
// §1.3 (manifest layout), §1.4 (pipeline), §1.5 (failure taxonomy),
// §1.9 (this file: the dual-gate structure and combined-verdict table).
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The one property this file exists to hold
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// A vendor firmware blob is trusted only if BOTH of the following are
// true:
//
//   (a) the vendor signed exactly these bytes, verified against the
//       vendor public key pinned in the keyring (keyring.pdx roles
//       vendor_intel / vendor_realtek), and
//   (b) Paideia release engineering re-signed the manifest describing
//       these bytes, verified against paideia_root.
//
// These are not redundant. (a) failing means the blob on disk is not
// the blob the vendor published — corruption or substitution. (b)
// failing means nobody at Paideia looked at this blob — it may be a
// genuine vendor artefact that simply was never reviewed. The two
// demand different operator responses, so the combined verdict names
// WHICH half failed rather than collapsing to a single "bad signature".
//
// The structural guarantee that neither gate can be skipped is not a
// comment, it is the shape of blob_verify_core: there is no control-flow
// edge from the vendor gate to any `ret`. The vendor gate falls through
// into the paideia gate unconditionally, both verdicts are recorded, and
// only then does blob_combine_verdicts — which reads both of its
// arguments on every path — produce an answer. A future edit that tried
// to short-circuit on a vendor failure would have to add a jump that is
// visibly absent today.
//
// A keyring miss is deliberately NOT an early return either. When
// keyring_lookup fails for a role, this file substitutes a null public
// key and RUNS THE GATE ANYWAY; driver_sig_verify_algo's gate 6 answers
// SIG_BAD_PK_PTR, which flows through the ordinary attribution machinery
// and lands on the correct half. That keeps the "both gates always run"
// invariant total, and costs one `xor`.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The scaffold epoch, restated for the combined verdict
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// sig_verify.pdx cannot return SIG_OK at R29 (see its header). It
// follows mechanically that blob_verify_core cannot return BLOB_OK at
// R29, because BLOB_OK requires v_vendor == SIG_OK AND v_paideia ==
// SIG_OK by exact equality. At R29 every well-formed all-zero signature
// yields SIG_UNSIGNED_SCAFFOLD on both halves, and the combined verdict
// is BLOB_UNSIGNED_SCAFFOLD — a value of its own, numerically distinct
// from BLOB_OK, meaning "structurally sound, provably UNSIGNED on both
// halves". blob_verdict_is_ok answers 0 for it, exactly as
// driver_sig_verdict_is_ok answers 0 for SIG_UNSIGNED_SCAFFOLD.
//
// R32 flips this in two edits and no more: sig_verify's terminal branch
// starts returning SIG_OK for genuinely verified material, and the
// call sites treat BLOB_UNSIGNED_SCAFFOLD as a hard reject. Nothing in
// the combination table changes.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The hash gate is a REAL gate with an ABSENT primitive
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Step 4 of the §1.4 pipeline is `h = sha3_256(blob); reject if h !=
// manifest.blob_hash`. There is no SHA3-256 anywhere in this tree —
// `grep -rniE "sha3|keccak" src/` returns design prose and one comment
// in keyring.pdx admitting the same gap. SHA3-256 is an R32 deliverable
// (src/kernel/core/crypto/, alongside the ML-DSA-65 lattice verifier),
// and hand-rolling a hash primitive here would be the same category of
// mistake sig_verify.pdx refuses to make with the lattice verifier: a
// subtly wrong Keccak that says "match" is worse than no hash at all.
//
// So the gate is split, and only the primitive is missing:
//
//   * blob_hash_eq        — the 32-byte comparison. Written, exercised.
//   * blob_hash_gate      — the policy: given a computed hash, compare;
//                           given none, DEFER while the scaffold epoch
//                           is live and FAIL CLOSED
//                           (BLOB_ERR_HASH_UNAVAILABLE) once it is not.
//   * blob_hash_compute   — the primitive. Returns
//                           BLOB_ERR_HASH_UNAVAILABLE at R29 and writes
//                           nothing. R32 replaces its body.
//
// The computed hash enters through a parameter rather than being fetched
// inside the gate (blob_load_verify_with_hash), which is what makes the
// comparison path testable today with a caller-supplied buffer and is
// also the shape R32 wants: the caller owns the scratch page the digest
// lands in. blob_load_verify is the 4-argument convenience wrapper that
// asks blob_hash_compute for that buffer and therefore passes 0 today.
//
// Fail-closed is the load-bearing half. If R32 lands the lattice
// verifier and forgets SHA3-256, the epoch flips to 0, the hash gate
// starts returning BLOB_ERR_HASH_UNAVAILABLE, and every blob load fails
// loudly — rather than a signature-verified blob whose bytes nobody
// compared against the manifest sailing through.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Manifest form
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// This file consumes the PARSED manifest (blob-policy §1.3.1): the
// widened, naturally-aligned in-memory form, 6784 bytes, produced from
// the packed on-disk `.pdxsig` bytes by the manifest reader that lands
// with the filesystem path at R32. The verifier deliberately does not
// parse packed attacker-controlled bytes itself — bit-extraction from
// hostile input belongs in exactly one reviewed place, and mixing it
// into the trust decision is how length-confusion bugs are born.
//
// Verdict numbering: 0xFFFFFDAx, one step past sig_verify's 0xFFFFFD8x,
// so a blob verdict is never confused with a signature verdict, a
// keyring miss, or a registry query failure where it surfaces in a log.

module BlobLoad = structure {

  // ============================================================================
  // Parsed-manifest layout (blob-policy §1.3.1). All offsets 8-aligned.
  //
  //     0  u64      magic            = 0x50445853 'PDXS'
  //     8  u64      version          = 1
  //    16  u64      paideia_algo_id  ML-DSA-65 = 1
  //    24  u64      vendor_algo_id   ML-DSA-65 = 1 at R29
  //    32  u64      vendor_role      keyring role: 1 intel, 2 realtek
  //    40  u64      hash_algo_id     SHA3-256 = 1
  //    48  u64      blob_len
  //    56  u64      timestamp
  //    64  u64      vendor_sig_len
  //    72  u64      paideia_sig_len
  //    80  [u8;32]  blob_hash        sha3_256(blob)
  //   112  [u8;16]  signer_pk_id
  //   128  [u8;16]  vendor_pk_id
  //   144  u64      reserved0
  //   152  u64      reserved1
  //   160  [u8;3312] vendor_sig      3309 used, 3 pad to keep the next
  //                                  field 8-aligned
  //  3472  [u8;3312] paideia_sig
  //  6784  end
  //
  // The Paideia signature covers bytes [0, 3472) — the whole header AND
  // the vendor signature, per §1.3. That is why vendor_sig precedes
  // paideia_sig and not the other way round: re-signing the vendor
  // signature is what stops an attacker pairing a genuine Paideia
  // manifest with a different vendor signature.
  // ============================================================================

  pub let BLOB_MANIFEST_MAGIC        : u64 = 0x50445853
  pub let BLOB_MANIFEST_VERSION      : u64 = 1
  pub let BLOB_MANIFEST_BYTES        : u64 = 6784
  pub let BLOB_MANIFEST_SIG_SLOT     : u64 = 3312

  pub let BLOB_OFF_MAGIC             : u64 = 0
  pub let BLOB_OFF_VERSION           : u64 = 8
  pub let BLOB_OFF_PAIDEIA_ALGO      : u64 = 16
  pub let BLOB_OFF_VENDOR_ALGO       : u64 = 24
  pub let BLOB_OFF_VENDOR_ROLE       : u64 = 32
  pub let BLOB_OFF_HASH_ALGO         : u64 = 40
  pub let BLOB_OFF_BLOB_LEN          : u64 = 48
  pub let BLOB_OFF_TIMESTAMP         : u64 = 56
  pub let BLOB_OFF_VENDOR_SIG_LEN    : u64 = 64
  pub let BLOB_OFF_PAIDEIA_SIG_LEN   : u64 = 72
  pub let BLOB_OFF_BLOB_HASH         : u64 = 80
  pub let BLOB_OFF_SIGNER_PK_ID      : u64 = 112
  pub let BLOB_OFF_VENDOR_PK_ID      : u64 = 128
  pub let BLOB_OFF_VENDOR_SIG        : u64 = 160
  pub let BLOB_OFF_PAIDEIA_SIG       : u64 = 3472

  // Bytes of the manifest the Paideia signature covers: everything up to
  // but excluding paideia_sig itself.
  pub let BLOB_PAIDEIA_SIGNED_BYTES  : u64 = 3472

  pub let BLOB_HASH_BYTES            : u64 = 32
  pub let BLOB_HASH_ALGO_SHA3_256    : u64 = 1

  // ============================================================================
  // Combined verdict taxonomy. Normative table: blob-policy §1.9.
  //
  // BLOB_OK                    both halves verified. UNREACHABLE AT R29 —
  //                            requires SIG_OK from both gates, and
  //                            sig_verify returns SIG_OK on no path.
  // BLOB_UNSIGNED_SCAFFOLD     both halves are well-formed and provably
  //                            UNSIGNED (all-zero). NOT a pass.
  // BLOB_ERR_VENDOR_SIG_BAD    the vendor half did not pass. The blob on
  //                            disk is not what the vendor published.
  // BLOB_ERR_PAIDEIA_SIG_BAD   the Paideia half did not pass. The blob
  //                            was never reviewed by Paideia release
  //                            engineering — INCLUDING the case where
  //                            the vendor signature alone is valid.
  // BLOB_ERR_BOTH_SIG_BAD      neither half passed.
  // ============================================================================

  pub let BLOB_OK                    : u64 = 0
  pub let BLOB_UNSIGNED_SCAFFOLD     : u64 = 1

  pub let BLOB_ERR_BAD_BLOB_PTR      : u64 = 0xFFFFFDA0
  pub let BLOB_ERR_BAD_BLOB_LEN      : u64 = 0xFFFFFDA1
  pub let BLOB_ERR_BAD_MANIFEST_PTR  : u64 = 0xFFFFFDA2
  pub let BLOB_ERR_BAD_MANIFEST_LEN  : u64 = 0xFFFFFDA3
  pub let BLOB_ERR_BAD_MAGIC         : u64 = 0xFFFFFDA4
  pub let BLOB_ERR_BAD_VERSION       : u64 = 0xFFFFFDA5
  pub let BLOB_ERR_BAD_HASH_ALGO     : u64 = 0xFFFFFDA6
  pub let BLOB_ERR_BAD_VENDOR_ROLE   : u64 = 0xFFFFFDA7
  pub let BLOB_ERR_LEN_MISMATCH      : u64 = 0xFFFFFDA8
  pub let BLOB_ERR_HASH_MISMATCH     : u64 = 0xFFFFFDA9
  pub let BLOB_ERR_HASH_UNAVAILABLE  : u64 = 0xFFFFFDAA
  pub let BLOB_ERR_VENDOR_SIG_BAD    : u64 = 0xFFFFFDAB
  pub let BLOB_ERR_PAIDEIA_SIG_BAD   : u64 = 0xFFFFFDAC
  pub let BLOB_ERR_BOTH_SIG_BAD      : u64 = 0xFFFFFDAD

  // ============================================================================
  // Gate identifiers. Answer to "which gate failed", carried in the
  // audit record (sig_telemetry.pdx) and surfaced to `safectl`.
  // ============================================================================

  pub let BLOB_GATE_NONE     : u64 = 0    // OK or scaffold — no gate failed
  pub let BLOB_GATE_ARGS     : u64 = 1    // blob pointer / length
  pub let BLOB_GATE_MANIFEST : u64 = 2    // manifest malformed
  pub let BLOB_GATE_HASH     : u64 = 3    // blob-vs-manifest identity
  pub let BLOB_GATE_VENDOR   : u64 = 4
  pub let BLOB_GATE_PAIDEIA  : u64 = 5
  pub let BLOB_GATE_BOTH     : u64 = 6
  pub let BLOB_GATE_UNKNOWN  : u64 = 7    // verdict not in the table

  // Hash-gate continuation codes. Both are <= 1 so the caller's
  // "continue" test is a single unsigned compare, matching the
  // convention that small values are outcomes and 0xFFFFFDxx values are
  // rejections.
  pub let BLOB_HASH_GATE_OK       : u64 = 0   // compared, equal
  pub let BLOB_HASH_GATE_DEFERRED : u64 = 1   // no digest, scaffold epoch

  // ============================================================================
  // Per-gate verdict record. Written by blob_verify_core on EVERY run
  // (including the runs that reject before the gates, where both are set
  // to a sentinel), read by sig_telemetry and by operators through
  // `safectl`. Kept here rather than returned because the combined
  // verdict is what call sites branch on, while the per-gate SIG_*
  // verdict is what a human debugging a rejection needs.
  //
  // Sentinel 0xFFFFFFFF = "this gate did not run" — distinct from every
  // SIG_* verdict, so "the vendor gate never executed" can never be
  // misread as "the vendor gate passed".
  // ============================================================================

  pub let BLOB_GATE_NOT_RUN : u64 = 0xFFFFFFFF

  pub let mut _blob_vendor_verdict  : u64 = 0xFFFFFFFF
  pub let mut _blob_paideia_verdict : u64 = 0xFFFFFFFF

  // R32 flips this to 1 in the same commit that lands SHA3-256.
  pub let mut _blob_hash_available : u64 = 0

  // ============================================================================
  // blob_hash_available() -> u64
  //
  //   -> rax = 1 once a SHA3-256 primitive exists, 0 at R29.
  // ============================================================================

  pub let blob_hash_available : () -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R29.M4-003 (#1034): read the SHA3-256 availability flag. rip-relative lea then one qword load. Leaf; no callee-save writes; no nested calls.",
      block: {
        lea rax, [rip + _blob_hash_available];
        mov rax, [rax + 0];
        ret
      }
    }

  // ============================================================================
  // blob_last_vendor_verdict() / blob_last_paideia_verdict() -> u64
  //
  //   -> rax = the SIG_* verdict the named gate produced on the most
  //            recent blob_verify_core run, or BLOB_GATE_NOT_RUN.
  // ============================================================================

  pub let blob_last_vendor_verdict : () -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R29.M4-003 (#1034): read the recorded vendor-gate verdict. rip-relative lea then one qword load. Leaf; no callee-save writes; no nested calls.",
      block: {
        lea rax, [rip + _blob_vendor_verdict];
        mov rax, [rax + 0];
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let blob_last_paideia_verdict : () -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R29.M4-003 (#1034): read the recorded paideia-gate verdict. rip-relative lea then one qword load. Leaf; no callee-save writes; no nested calls.",
      block: {
        lea rax, [rip + _blob_paideia_verdict];
        mov rax, [rax + 0];
        ret
      }
    }

  // ============================================================================
  // blob_verdict_is_ok(verdict: u64) -> u64
  //
  //   -> rax = 1 if and only if verdict == BLOB_OK (0).
  //
  // The same contract, and the same reason for existing, as
  // driver_sig_verdict_is_ok: it is the ONLY sanctioned way to turn a
  // combined verdict into an accept/reject decision. BLOB_UNSIGNED_SCAFFOLD
  // (1) answers 0 here, so the natural-but-catastrophic `cmp rax, 1; jbe
  // accept` never gets written at a call site.
  // ============================================================================

  pub let blob_verdict_is_ok : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (verdict: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R29.M4-003 (#1034): exact-equality predicate against BLOB_OK (0). xor rax,rax then cmp rdi,0 / jne out sets 1 only on exact zero, so every other value — BLOB_UNSIGNED_SCAFFOLD (1) included — answers 0. No memory touched, no effects. Leaf; no callee-save writes; no nested calls.",
      block: {
        xor rax, rax;
        cmp rdi, 0;
        jne blob_verdict_is_ok_out;
        mov rax, 1;

      blob_verdict_is_ok_out:
        ret
      }
    }

  // ============================================================================
  // blob_gate_clean(sig_verdict: u64) -> u64
  //
  //   -> rax = 1 if the gate verdict is SIG_OK (0) or
  //            SIG_UNSIGNED_SCAFFOLD (1), else 0.
  //
  // READ THIS BEFORE USING IT. "Clean" is NOT "acceptable". It answers
  // one narrow question: did this gate reach a terminal, structurally
  // sound conclusion about a well-formed signature field, as opposed to
  // rejecting the field outright? It exists purely so that
  // blob_combine_verdicts can ATTRIBUTE a failure to the right half
  // during the scaffold epoch, when the paideia half is permanently
  // SIG_UNSIGNED_SCAFFOLD and a vendor-half malformation would otherwise
  // be indistinguishable from a double failure.
  //
  // Acceptance never routes through this predicate. blob_combine_verdicts
  // produces BLOB_OK only from two EXACT equalities against SIG_OK, and
  // call sites decide via blob_verdict_is_ok. If this function were
  // deleted the accept/reject behaviour of the system would not change;
  // only the precision of the failure code would.
  //
  // Written as two exact compares rather than `cmp rdi, 1; jbe` so that
  // no value other than 0 and 1 can ever answer 1, whatever the SIG_*
  // numbering grows into.
  // ============================================================================

  pub let blob_gate_clean : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (sig_verdict: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R29.M4-003 (#1034): membership test for {SIG_OK, SIG_UNSIGNED_SCAFFOLD} by two exact compares (never an unsigned range test — see the header note on why a range test here would be a latent accept-unsigned bug if the SIG_* numbering ever grew). Attribution helper only; acceptance is decided by blob_verdict_is_ok on the combined verdict. No memory touched, no effects. Leaf; no callee-save writes; no nested calls.",
      block: {
        xor rax, rax;
        cmp rdi, 0;
        je  blob_gate_clean_yes;
        cmp rdi, 1;
        jne blob_gate_clean_out;

      blob_gate_clean_yes:
        mov rax, 1;

      blob_gate_clean_out:
        ret
      }
    }

  // ============================================================================
  // blob_failed_gate(verdict: u64) -> u64
  //
  //   -> rax = the BLOB_GATE_* identifier the combined verdict implicates.
  //
  // Pure mapping, no state. Carried into the audit record so an operator
  // reading /system/audit/log.pdaudit sees "which half" without a lookup
  // table in their head, and so a dashboard can bucket rejections by
  // stage. Total: any verdict outside the table answers
  // BLOB_GATE_UNKNOWN rather than BLOB_GATE_NONE — an unrecognised
  // failure must never be reported as "no gate failed".
  // ============================================================================

  pub let blob_failed_gate : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (verdict: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R29.M4-003 (#1034): verdict -> gate-id mapping as a descending compare chain over the 0xFFFFFDAx block. BLOB_OK (0) and BLOB_UNSIGNED_SCAFFOLD (1) map to BLOB_GATE_NONE; the argument/manifest/hash/vendor/paideia/both blocks map to their gate ids; anything unmapped maps to BLOB_GATE_UNKNOWN (never NONE — an unrecognised failure reported as 'no gate failed' is exactly the misread this default exists to prevent). Constants materialised into r10 before each compare, matching the 0xFFFFFDxx convention in registry_lookup.pdx. No memory touched, no effects. Leaf; no callee-save writes; no nested calls. Labels prefixed blob_failed_gate_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 0;
        je  blob_failed_gate_none;
        cmp rdi, 1;
        je  blob_failed_gate_none;

        mov r10, 0xFFFFFDA0;                 // BLOB_ERR_BAD_BLOB_PTR
        cmp rdi, r10;
        je  blob_failed_gate_args;
        mov r10, 0xFFFFFDA1;                 // BLOB_ERR_BAD_BLOB_LEN
        cmp rdi, r10;
        je  blob_failed_gate_args;

        mov r10, 0xFFFFFDA2;                 // BLOB_ERR_BAD_MANIFEST_PTR
        cmp rdi, r10;
        je  blob_failed_gate_manifest;
        mov r10, 0xFFFFFDA3;                 // BLOB_ERR_BAD_MANIFEST_LEN
        cmp rdi, r10;
        je  blob_failed_gate_manifest;
        mov r10, 0xFFFFFDA4;                 // BLOB_ERR_BAD_MAGIC
        cmp rdi, r10;
        je  blob_failed_gate_manifest;
        mov r10, 0xFFFFFDA5;                 // BLOB_ERR_BAD_VERSION
        cmp rdi, r10;
        je  blob_failed_gate_manifest;
        mov r10, 0xFFFFFDA6;                 // BLOB_ERR_BAD_HASH_ALGO
        cmp rdi, r10;
        je  blob_failed_gate_manifest;
        mov r10, 0xFFFFFDA7;                 // BLOB_ERR_BAD_VENDOR_ROLE
        cmp rdi, r10;
        je  blob_failed_gate_manifest;

        mov r10, 0xFFFFFDA8;                 // BLOB_ERR_LEN_MISMATCH
        cmp rdi, r10;
        je  blob_failed_gate_hash;
        mov r10, 0xFFFFFDA9;                 // BLOB_ERR_HASH_MISMATCH
        cmp rdi, r10;
        je  blob_failed_gate_hash;
        mov r10, 0xFFFFFDAA;                 // BLOB_ERR_HASH_UNAVAILABLE
        cmp rdi, r10;
        je  blob_failed_gate_hash;

        mov r10, 0xFFFFFDAB;                 // BLOB_ERR_VENDOR_SIG_BAD
        cmp rdi, r10;
        je  blob_failed_gate_vendor;
        mov r10, 0xFFFFFDAC;                 // BLOB_ERR_PAIDEIA_SIG_BAD
        cmp rdi, r10;
        je  blob_failed_gate_paideia;
        mov r10, 0xFFFFFDAD;                 // BLOB_ERR_BOTH_SIG_BAD
        cmp rdi, r10;
        je  blob_failed_gate_both;

        mov rax, 7;                          // BLOB_GATE_UNKNOWN
        ret;

      blob_failed_gate_none:
        xor rax, rax;                        // BLOB_GATE_NONE
        ret;

      blob_failed_gate_args:
        mov rax, 1;                          // BLOB_GATE_ARGS
        ret;

      blob_failed_gate_manifest:
        mov rax, 2;                          // BLOB_GATE_MANIFEST
        ret;

      blob_failed_gate_hash:
        mov rax, 3;                          // BLOB_GATE_HASH
        ret;

      blob_failed_gate_vendor:
        mov rax, 4;                          // BLOB_GATE_VENDOR
        ret;

      blob_failed_gate_paideia:
        mov rax, 5;                          // BLOB_GATE_PAIDEIA
        ret;

      blob_failed_gate_both:
        mov rax, 6;                          // BLOB_GATE_BOTH
        ret
      }
    }

  // ============================================================================
  // blob_hash_eq(a_ptr: u64, b_ptr: u64) -> u64
  //
  //   -> rax = 1 if the 32 bytes at a_ptr equal the 32 bytes at b_ptr.
  //            A null on either side answers 0 without dereferencing —
  //            "could not compare" must never read as "compared equal",
  //            the same principle driver_sig_all_zero rests on.
  //
  // Four qword compares; 32 is an exact multiple of 8 so there is no
  // tail, and no tail means no off-by-one in the place where an
  // off-by-one would let a tampered blob match a stale manifest.
  //
  // Not constant-time, deliberately: both operands are public — one is
  // the manifest's declared digest, the other is a digest of bytes the
  // caller already holds. Neither is a secret, and there is no key
  // material within a mile of this comparison.
  // ============================================================================

  pub let blob_hash_eq : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (a_ptr: u64, b_ptr: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R29.M4-003 (#1034): 32-byte equality as four qword compares (32 is an exact multiple of 8 — no tail, hence no off-by-one). Null on either side returns 0 without dereferencing. r8/r9 walk the two buffers, rcx counts 4 qwords down; all caller-save, so no prologue. Read-only; writes no memory. Leaf; no nested calls. Labels prefixed blob_hash_eq_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        xor rax, rax;

        cmp rdi, 0;
        je  blob_hash_eq_out;
        cmp rsi, 0;
        je  blob_hash_eq_out;

        mov r8,  rdi;
        mov r9,  rsi;
        mov rcx, 4;                          // BLOB_HASH_BYTES / 8

      blob_hash_eq_scan:
        cmp rcx, 0;
        je  blob_hash_eq_same;
        mov r10, [r8 + 0];
        mov r11, [r9 + 0];
        cmp r10, r11;
        jne blob_hash_eq_out;                // rax already 0
        add r8, 8;
        add r9, 8;
        sub rcx, 1;
        jmp blob_hash_eq_scan;

      blob_hash_eq_same:
        mov rax, 1;

      blob_hash_eq_out:
        ret
      }
    }

  // ============================================================================
  // blob_hash_compute(blob_ptr, blob_len, out_ptr) -> u64
  //
  //   -> rax = BLOB_ERR_HASH_UNAVAILABLE at R29. Writes NOTHING to
  //            out_ptr.
  //
  // The SHA3-256 primitive, absent. This is not a stub that pretends;
  // it is a declared dependency with a call signature fixed now so R32
  // replaces a body and not an interface. Returning a failure code and
  // leaving out_ptr untouched is the only honest behaviour: a function
  // that zero-filled out_ptr would hand its caller a plausible-looking
  // digest that matches an all-zero manifest field, which is precisely
  // the "scaffold becomes a permanent hole" failure sig_verify.pdx was
  // written to avoid.
  //
  // R32: src/kernel/core/crypto/sha3/sha3_256.pdx, together with the
  // ML-DSA-65 lattice verifier. Flip _blob_hash_available to 1 in the
  // same commit.
  // ============================================================================

  pub let blob_hash_compute : (u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (blob_ptr: u64, blob_len: u64, out_ptr: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R29.M4-003 (#1034): declared R32 dependency on SHA3-256. Returns BLOB_ERR_HASH_UNAVAILABLE and writes nothing — deliberately NOT a zero-fill, which would manufacture a digest matching an all-zero manifest field. The {mem} effect is declared for the R32 body that will write out_ptr, so the effect signature does not change when the primitive lands. Leaf; no callee-save writes; no nested calls.",
      block: {
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFDAA;                 // BLOB_ERR_HASH_UNAVAILABLE
        ret
      }
    }

  // ============================================================================
  // blob_hash_gate(manifest_ptr: u64, computed_hash_ptr: u64) -> u64
  //
  //   -> rax = BLOB_HASH_GATE_OK (0)        digest supplied and equal
  //            BLOB_HASH_GATE_DEFERRED (1)  no digest, scaffold epoch live
  //            BLOB_ERR_HASH_MISMATCH       digest supplied and different
  //            BLOB_ERR_HASH_UNAVAILABLE    no digest, scaffold epoch OVER
  //            BLOB_ERR_BAD_MANIFEST_PTR    null manifest
  //
  // Step 4 of the §1.4 pipeline. The deferral is bounded by exactly one
  // condition — driver_sig_scaffold_epoch() == 1 — and that condition is
  // the same flag R32 clears when it lands the real primitives. So the
  // day the epoch ends, a still-missing SHA3-256 turns every blob load
  // into a loud BLOB_ERR_HASH_UNAVAILABLE rejection rather than a quiet
  // skipped step. Fail-closed by construction, not by remembering.
  // ============================================================================

  pub let blob_hash_gate : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (manifest_ptr: u64, computed_hash_ptr: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R29.M4-003 (#1034): the blob-identity gate. Null manifest rejected before any deref. With no computed digest the outcome is governed solely by driver_sig_scaffold_epoch(): 1 defers, 0 fails closed with BLOB_ERR_HASH_UNAVAILABLE — the flag R32 clears is the same flag that ends the deferral, so the fail-closed flip needs no separate edit. With a digest, blob_hash_eq compares it against manifest.blob_hash at offset 80. Single push rbx: at entry rsp%16==8, one push brings it to 0 as SysV requires before the two nested calls; nothing needs to survive them but rax, and rbx carries manifest_ptr across the epoch call so the comparison arm can still reach the digest field. Labels prefixed blob_hash_gate_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        push rbx;                            // rsp%16 = 0 (pad + save)
        mov rbx, rdi;                        // manifest_ptr survives calls

        cmp rbx, 0;
        je  blob_hash_gate_bad_manifest;

        cmp rsi, 0;
        jne blob_hash_gate_compare;

        // ---- No digest available ----
        call driver_sig_scaffold_epoch;
        cmp rax, 1;
        jne blob_hash_gate_unavailable;
        mov rax, 1;                          // BLOB_HASH_GATE_DEFERRED
        jmp blob_hash_gate_ret;

      blob_hash_gate_compare:
        mov rdi, rbx;
        add rdi, 80;                         // BLOB_OFF_BLOB_HASH
        // rsi already = computed_hash_ptr
        call blob_hash_eq;
        cmp rax, 1;
        jne blob_hash_gate_mismatch;
        xor rax, rax;                        // BLOB_HASH_GATE_OK
        jmp blob_hash_gate_ret;

      blob_hash_gate_mismatch:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFDA9;                 // BLOB_ERR_HASH_MISMATCH
        jmp blob_hash_gate_ret;

      blob_hash_gate_unavailable:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFDAA;                 // BLOB_ERR_HASH_UNAVAILABLE
        jmp blob_hash_gate_ret;

      blob_hash_gate_bad_manifest:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFDA2;                 // BLOB_ERR_BAD_MANIFEST_PTR

      blob_hash_gate_ret:
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ============================================================================
  // blob_manifest_check(manifest_ptr, manifest_len, blob_len) -> u64
  //
  //   -> rax = 0 if the manifest is structurally usable, else the
  //            BLOB_ERR_* code for the first property that failed.
  //
  // Gate order is load-bearing in the same way sig_verify's is: the
  // length gate precedes every dereference, so a short buffer produces
  // BLOB_ERR_BAD_MANIFEST_LEN rather than a read past its end.
  //
  //   1. manifest_ptr != 0        -> BLOB_ERR_BAD_MANIFEST_PTR
  //   2. manifest_len == 6784     -> BLOB_ERR_BAD_MANIFEST_LEN
  //   3. magic == 'PDXS'          -> BLOB_ERR_BAD_MAGIC
  //   4. version == 1             -> BLOB_ERR_BAD_VERSION
  //   5. hash_algo == SHA3-256    -> BLOB_ERR_BAD_HASH_ALGO
  //   6. vendor_role in {1, 2}    -> BLOB_ERR_BAD_VENDOR_ROLE
  //   7. manifest.blob_len == blob_len -> BLOB_ERR_LEN_MISMATCH
  //
  // Gate 6 excludes role 0 (paideia_root) as deliberately as it excludes
  // role 3 (nonexistent). A manifest naming paideia_root as the VENDOR
  // key would make the two gates verify the same signature against the
  // same anchor, collapsing the dual signature into a single one — the
  // exact confusion D1.a exists to forbid, and a one-byte edit away in
  // an unchecked field.
  //
  // The two signature LENGTHS are not checked here on purpose. They are
  // per-half properties, and driver_sig_verify_algo's gate 5 already
  // rejects them with SIG_BAD_LENGTH — which flows through the
  // attribution machinery and names the offending half. Checking them
  // here would collapse "the vendor signature is the wrong length" and
  // "the Paideia signature is the wrong length" into one code, throwing
  // away the very distinction this file is about.
  // ============================================================================

  pub let blob_manifest_check : (u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (manifest_ptr: u64, manifest_len: u64, blob_len: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R29.M4-003 (#1034): seven structural gates over the parsed manifest. Pointer then length then content, so no field is read before the 6784-byte extent is proven — a short manifest yields BLOB_ERR_BAD_MANIFEST_LEN, never an over-read. Gate 6 admits only roles 1 and 2; role 0 (paideia_root) is refused because a manifest naming the Paideia root as its vendor key would verify both halves against the same anchor and collapse the dual signature into a single one. Signature lengths are intentionally NOT checked here — they are per-half and belong to the gates, which attribute them. Constants materialised via mov r10, imm32 then cmp. Read-only; writes no memory. Leaf; no nested calls. Labels prefixed blob_manifest_check_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        // ---- Gate 1: manifest pointer ----
        cmp rdi, 0;
        je  blob_manifest_check_bad_ptr;

        // ---- Gate 2: manifest length (BEFORE any deref) ----
        mov r10, 6784;                       // BLOB_MANIFEST_BYTES
        cmp rsi, r10;
        jne blob_manifest_check_bad_len;

        // ---- Gate 3: magic ----
        mov rax, [rdi + 0];                  // BLOB_OFF_MAGIC
        mov r10, 0x50445853;                 // 'PDXS'
        cmp rax, r10;
        jne blob_manifest_check_bad_magic;

        // ---- Gate 4: version ----
        mov rax, [rdi + 8];                  // BLOB_OFF_VERSION
        cmp rax, 1;
        jne blob_manifest_check_bad_version;

        // ---- Gate 5: hash algorithm ----
        mov rax, [rdi + 40];                 // BLOB_OFF_HASH_ALGO
        cmp rax, 1;                          // BLOB_HASH_ALGO_SHA3_256
        jne blob_manifest_check_bad_hash_algo;

        // ---- Gate 6: vendor role is a VENDOR role ----
        mov rax, [rdi + 32];                 // BLOB_OFF_VENDOR_ROLE
        cmp rax, 1;                          // KEYRING_ROLE_VENDOR_INTEL
        je  blob_manifest_check_role_ok;
        cmp rax, 2;                          // KEYRING_ROLE_VENDOR_REALTEK
        jne blob_manifest_check_bad_role;

      blob_manifest_check_role_ok:
        // ---- Gate 7: declared blob length agrees with the caller's ----
        mov rax, [rdi + 48];                 // BLOB_OFF_BLOB_LEN
        cmp rax, rdx;
        jne blob_manifest_check_len_mismatch;

        xor rax, rax;
        ret;

      blob_manifest_check_bad_ptr:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFDA2;
        ret;

      blob_manifest_check_bad_len:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFDA3;
        ret;

      blob_manifest_check_bad_magic:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFDA4;
        ret;

      blob_manifest_check_bad_version:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFDA5;
        ret;

      blob_manifest_check_bad_hash_algo:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFDA6;
        ret;

      blob_manifest_check_bad_role:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFDA7;
        ret;

      blob_manifest_check_len_mismatch:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFDA8;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ============================================================================
  // blob_combine_verdicts(v_vendor: u64, v_paideia: u64) -> u64
  //
  // THE dual-signature rule, in one total function.
  //
  //   v_vendor == SIG_OK   AND v_paideia == SIG_OK    -> BLOB_OK
  //   v_vendor == SCAFFOLD AND v_paideia == SCAFFOLD  -> BLOB_UNSIGNED_SCAFFOLD
  //   neither half clean                              -> BLOB_ERR_BOTH_SIG_BAD
  //   vendor half not clean                           -> BLOB_ERR_VENDOR_SIG_BAD
  //   paideia half not clean                          -> BLOB_ERR_PAIDEIA_SIG_BAD
  //   both clean but mixed (one OK, one unsigned)     -> the UNSIGNED half's code
  //
  // The last line is the single-valid-signature rule §1.1 states three
  // times, and it is worth being explicit about because it is the case
  // an attacker constructs. A blob carrying a genuine vendor signature
  // and no Paideia re-sign yields BLOB_ERR_PAIDEIA_SIG_BAD. A blob
  // carrying a genuine Paideia manifest and no vendor signature yields
  // BLOB_ERR_VENDOR_SIG_BAD. Neither yields BLOB_OK, and neither yields
  // BLOB_UNSIGNED_SCAFFOLD — that verdict is reserved for the honest
  // case where BOTH halves are absent, which is what the whole tree
  // looks like today and must not be confused with a half-signed blob.
  //
  // BLOB_OK requires two exact equalities against SIG_OK. Since no path
  // in sig_verify.pdx returns SIG_OK at R29, no call to this function
  // from blob_verify_core can produce BLOB_OK at R29. The arm exists so
  // R32 needs no edit here; the boot witness pins that it is reachable
  // ONLY from synthetic arguments.
  // ============================================================================

  pub let blob_combine_verdicts : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (v_vendor: u64, v_paideia: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R29.M4-003 (#1034): the dual-signature combination table as a total branch tree. Both arguments are read on every path — there is no arm that inspects one half and returns, which is the structural encoding of 'neither gate can be skipped'. Cleanliness of each half is computed inline into r10/r11 by two exact compares each (never a range test; see blob_gate_clean's header). Decision order: both-exactly-OK, both-exactly-SCAFFOLD, both-unclean, vendor-unclean, paideia-unclean, then the mixed clean case where the SCAFFOLD half is named. BLOB_OK is produced only by two exact equalities against SIG_OK, which is why it is unreachable at R29 by construction rather than by convention. No memory touched, no effects. Leaf; no callee-save writes; no nested calls. Labels prefixed blob_combine_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        // ---- clean(v_vendor) -> r10 ----
        xor r10, r10;
        cmp rdi, 0;
        je  blob_combine_v_clean;
        cmp rdi, 1;
        jne blob_combine_v_done;

      blob_combine_v_clean:
        mov r10, 1;

      blob_combine_v_done:
        // ---- clean(v_paideia) -> r11 ----
        xor r11, r11;
        cmp rsi, 0;
        je  blob_combine_p_clean;
        cmp rsi, 1;
        jne blob_combine_p_done;

      blob_combine_p_clean:
        mov r11, 1;

      blob_combine_p_done:
        // ---- Rule 1: both halves verified. Unreachable at R29 ----
        cmp rdi, 0;                          // SIG_OK
        jne blob_combine_r2;
        cmp rsi, 0;                          // SIG_OK
        jne blob_combine_r2;
        xor rax, rax;                        // BLOB_OK
        ret;

      blob_combine_r2:
        // ---- Rule 2: both halves provably unsigned ----
        cmp rdi, 1;                          // SIG_UNSIGNED_SCAFFOLD
        jne blob_combine_r3;
        cmp rsi, 1;                          // SIG_UNSIGNED_SCAFFOLD
        jne blob_combine_r3;
        mov rax, 1;                          // BLOB_UNSIGNED_SCAFFOLD
        ret;

      blob_combine_r3:
        // ---- Rule 3: neither half reached a sound conclusion ----
        cmp r10, 0;
        jne blob_combine_r4;
        cmp r11, 0;
        jne blob_combine_vendor_bad;
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFDAD;                 // BLOB_ERR_BOTH_SIG_BAD
        ret;

      blob_combine_r4:
        // ---- vendor clean here; is paideia? ----
        cmp r11, 0;
        je  blob_combine_paideia_bad;

        // ---- Rule 6: both clean, mixed. Name the UNSIGNED half ----
        cmp rdi, 1;                          // vendor is the unsigned one
        je  blob_combine_vendor_bad;
        jmp blob_combine_paideia_bad;

      blob_combine_vendor_bad:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFDAB;                 // BLOB_ERR_VENDOR_SIG_BAD
        ret;

      blob_combine_paideia_bad:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFDAC;                 // BLOB_ERR_PAIDEIA_SIG_BAD
        ret
      }
    }

  // ============================================================================
  // blob_verify_core(blob_ptr, blob_len, manifest_ptr, manifest_len,
  //                  computed_hash_ptr) -> u64
  //
  //   rdi = blob bytes
  //   rsi = blob length
  //   rdx = parsed manifest (6784 B)
  //   rcx = manifest length (must be 6784)
  //   r8  = 32-byte digest of the blob, or 0 if none is available
  //   -> rax = the combined verdict.
  //
  // Verdict computation ONLY. It records nothing to the audit log.
  // Callers MUST use blob_load_verify / blob_load_verify_with_hash,
  // which are the only entry points that also emit the audit record —
  // this one is public solely so the boot witness and the R32 unit tests
  // can drive the verdict table without a log side effect.
  //
  // Pipeline (blob-policy §1.4, adapted to what exists at R29):
  //
  //   1. blob pointer / length
  //   2. manifest structure          (blob_manifest_check)
  //   3. blob identity               (blob_hash_gate)
  //   4. VENDOR GATE      -- always runs
  //   5. PAIDEIA GATE     -- always runs, no edge skips it
  //   6. combine
  //
  // Steps 4 and 5 have no `ret` between them. That is the enforcement.
  //
  // Note the ORDER of 4 and 5 relative to §1.4, which verifies Paideia
  // first. The order is immaterial here precisely BECAUSE neither gate
  // can short-circuit the other: §1.4's ordering was a fail-fast
  // optimisation, and this file does not fail fast between the halves.
  // Vendor is evaluated first only so the register carrying its verdict
  // (rbx) is established before the second call clobbers rax.
  // ============================================================================

  pub let blob_verify_core : (u64, u64, u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (blob_ptr: u64, blob_len: u64, manifest_ptr: u64,
        manifest_len: u64, computed_hash_ptr: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R29.M4-003 (#1034): the dual-gate verifier. Five-push prologue (rbx, r12-r15): at entry rsp%16==8 and five pushes bring it to 0, which SysV requires before each of the five nested calls (blob_manifest_check, blob_hash_gate, keyring_lookup x2, driver_sig_verify_algo x2 — all at the same depth). r12=blob_ptr, r13=blob_len, r14=manifest_ptr, r15=computed_hash_ptr, rbx=vendor-gate verdict; every one of these must survive a call, hence callee-save. Both per-gate verdicts are seeded to BLOB_GATE_NOT_RUN before any gate, so an early rejection cannot leave a stale verdict from a previous call readable as this call's result. Structural gates reject before the sig gates; the hash gate's continuation codes are <=1 so 'continue' is one unsigned compare (ja). Keyring misses are normalised to a null pk and the gate is CALLED ANYWAY — driver_sig_verify_algo gate 6 answers SIG_BAD_PK_PTR, which keeps 'both gates always run' total without a special case. The vendor gate falls through into the paideia gate with no intervening ret: that absence is the structural encoding of D1.a. Read-only w.r.t. its arguments; writes only the two verdict slots. Labels prefixed blob_verify_core_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;                            // 5 pushes -> rsp%16 = 0

        mov r12, rdi;                        // blob_ptr
        mov r13, rsi;                        // blob_len
        mov r14, rdx;                        // manifest_ptr
        mov r15, r8;                         // computed_hash_ptr

        // Seed both gate verdicts to "did not run" so an early rejection
        // never leaves the previous call's verdicts readable as this
        // call's.
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFF;                 // BLOB_GATE_NOT_RUN
        lea r10, [rip + _blob_vendor_verdict];
        mov [r10 + 0], rax;
        lea r10, [rip + _blob_paideia_verdict];
        mov [r10 + 0], rax;

        // ---- Step 1: blob pointer / length ----
        cmp r12, 0;
        je  blob_verify_core_bad_blob_ptr;
        cmp r13, 0;
        je  blob_verify_core_bad_blob_len;

        // ---- Step 2: manifest structure ----
        mov rdi, r14;
        mov rsi, rcx;                        // manifest_len
        mov rdx, r13;                        // blob_len
        call blob_manifest_check;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne blob_verify_core_ret;            // propagate the BLOB_ERR_* code

        // ---- Step 3: blob identity ----
        mov rdi, r14;
        mov rsi, r15;
        call blob_hash_gate;
        cmp rax, 1;                          // OK (0) / DEFERRED (1) continue
        ja  blob_verify_core_ret;            // every rejection is > 1

        // ==== Step 4: VENDOR GATE =============================
        // Resolve the pinned vendor key. A miss is normalised to a null
        // key and the gate runs regardless — see the header.
        mov rdi, [r14 + 32];                 // BLOB_OFF_VENDOR_ROLE
        call keyring_lookup;
        mov r10, 0xFFFFFD60;                 // KEYRING_ERR_NO_SUCH_KEY
        cmp rax, r10;
        jne blob_verify_core_vendor_key_ok;
        xor rax, rax;                        // -> gate 6: SIG_BAD_PK_PTR

      blob_verify_core_vendor_key_ok:
        mov r9,  rax;                        // pk_ptr
        mov rdi, [r14 + 24];                 // BLOB_OFF_VENDOR_ALGO
        mov rsi, r12;                        // artifact = the blob itself
        mov rdx, r13;                        // artifact_len = blob_len
        mov rcx, r14;
        add rcx, 160;                        // BLOB_OFF_VENDOR_SIG
        mov r8,  [r14 + 64];                 // BLOB_OFF_VENDOR_SIG_LEN
        call driver_sig_verify_algo;
        mov rbx, rax;                        // vendor verdict
        lea r10, [rip + _blob_vendor_verdict];
        mov [r10 + 0], rbx;

        // ==== Step 5: PAIDEIA GATE ===========================
        // No `ret` sits between step 4 and here. Do not add one.
        mov rdi, 0;                          // KEYRING_ROLE_PAIDEIA_ROOT
        call keyring_lookup;
        mov r10, 0xFFFFFD60;                 // KEYRING_ERR_NO_SUCH_KEY
        cmp rax, r10;
        jne blob_verify_core_paideia_key_ok;
        xor rax, rax;                        // -> gate 6: SIG_BAD_PK_PTR

      blob_verify_core_paideia_key_ok:
        mov r9,  rax;                        // pk_ptr = paideia_root
        mov rdi, [r14 + 16];                 // BLOB_OFF_PAIDEIA_ALGO
        mov rsi, r14;                        // artifact = manifest prefix
        mov rdx, 3472;                       // BLOB_PAIDEIA_SIGNED_BYTES
        mov rcx, r14;
        add rcx, 3472;                       // BLOB_OFF_PAIDEIA_SIG
        mov r8,  [r14 + 72];                 // BLOB_OFF_PAIDEIA_SIG_LEN
        call driver_sig_verify_algo;
        lea r10, [rip + _blob_paideia_verdict];
        mov [r10 + 0], rax;

        // ---- Step 6: combine. Reads BOTH halves ----
        mov rdi, rbx;                        // vendor verdict
        mov rsi, rax;                        // paideia verdict
        call blob_combine_verdicts;
        jmp blob_verify_core_ret;

      blob_verify_core_bad_blob_ptr:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFDA0;
        jmp blob_verify_core_ret;

      blob_verify_core_bad_blob_len:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFDA1;

      blob_verify_core_ret:
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ============================================================================
  // blob_load_verify_with_hash(blob_ptr, blob_len, manifest_ptr,
  //                            manifest_len, computed_hash_ptr) -> u64
  //
  // The sanctioned entry point when the caller already holds a digest of
  // the blob. Runs blob_verify_core and then records the outcome through
  // blob_sig_audit — pass, scaffold, and every failure mode, at the level
  // the outcome deserves. Recording is inside the entry point rather than
  // at the call sites because "every verification outcome is audited"
  // (#1035) is a property of the verifier, and a property that depends on
  // N call sites remembering is not a property.
  // ============================================================================

  pub let blob_load_verify_with_hash : (u64, u64, u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{sysreg, mem} @{} =
    fn (blob_ptr: u64, blob_len: u64, manifest_ptr: u64,
        manifest_len: u64, computed_hash_ptr: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {sysreg, mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R29.M4-003 (#1034) / R29.M4-004 (#1035): verify-then-record. Three-push prologue (rbx, r12, r13): at entry rsp%16==8, three pushes bring it to 0 as SysV requires before both nested calls; r13 is pushed for alignment as much as for use. rbx holds the combined verdict across the audit call; r12 holds manifest_ptr, which blob_sig_audit reads blob_len and the hash prefix from. The five incoming argument registers pass straight through to blob_verify_core untouched, so no shuffling is needed before that call. Effects are {sysreg, mem} because the audit path reaches kread_tsc (rdtsc) and klog; blob_verify_core itself is {mem}. rax carries the verdict back unchanged — auditing never alters the answer. Labels prefixed blob_load_verify_with_hash_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push r12;
        push r13;                            // 3 pushes -> rsp%16 = 0

        mov r12, rdx;                        // manifest_ptr (for the record)

        call blob_verify_core;
        mov rbx, rax;                        // combined verdict

        // Record: verdict, both gate verdicts, vendor role, manifest.
        mov rdi, rbx;
        lea r10, [rip + _blob_vendor_verdict];
        mov rsi, [r10 + 0];
        lea r10, [rip + _blob_paideia_verdict];
        mov rdx, [r10 + 0];
        xor rcx, rcx;                        // vendor role, 0 if no manifest
        cmp r12, 0;
        je  blob_load_verify_with_hash_emit;
        mov rcx, [r12 + 32];                 // BLOB_OFF_VENDOR_ROLE

      blob_load_verify_with_hash_emit:
        mov r8, r12;                         // manifest_ptr
        call blob_sig_audit;

        mov rax, rbx;                        // verdict unchanged by auditing
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ============================================================================
  // blob_load_verify(blob_ptr, blob_len, manifest_ptr, manifest_len) -> u64
  //
  // The entry point named by issue #1034, and the one drivers call.
  // Obtains the blob digest from blob_hash_compute and forwards.
  //
  // At R29 blob_hash_compute has no primitive behind it, so this wrapper
  // forwards a null digest and the hash gate defers (and, once the
  // scaffold epoch ends without SHA3-256, fails closed). The wrapper is
  // written so that R32 changes nothing here: when blob_hash_compute
  // starts filling the scratch buffer and returning success, the same
  // code path hands the real digest to the same gate.
  // ============================================================================

  pub let mut _blob_hash_scratch : [u64; 4] = [0; 4] @align(8)

  pub let blob_load_verify : (u64, u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{sysreg, mem} @{} =
    fn (blob_ptr: u64, blob_len: u64, manifest_ptr: u64, manifest_len: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {sysreg, mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R29.M4-003 (#1034): four-argument wrapper. Asks blob_hash_compute for a digest of the blob into _blob_hash_scratch; on success (R32) forwards the scratch address as the fifth argument, on BLOB_ERR_HASH_UNAVAILABLE (R29) forwards 0 so blob_hash_gate takes its defer-or-fail-closed branch. Five-push prologue (rbx, r12-r15): at entry rsp%16==8, five pushes bring it to 0 as SysV requires before both nested calls. r12=blob_ptr, r13=blob_len, r14=manifest_ptr, r15=manifest_len — all four must survive blob_hash_compute, hence callee-save; rbx carries the digest address (or 0) across the branch. rax carries the forwarded verdict back untouched. Labels prefixed blob_load_verify_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;                            // 5 pushes -> rsp%16 = 0

        mov r12, rdi;                        // blob_ptr
        mov r13, rsi;                        // blob_len
        mov r14, rdx;                        // manifest_ptr
        mov r15, rcx;                        // manifest_len

        // Digest the blob into the scratch buffer, if we can.
        mov rdi, r12;
        mov rsi, r13;
        lea rdx, [rip + _blob_hash_scratch];
        call blob_hash_compute;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  blob_load_verify_have_hash;
        xor rbx, rbx;                        // no digest available
        jmp blob_load_verify_go;

      blob_load_verify_have_hash:
        lea rbx, [rip + _blob_hash_scratch];

      blob_load_verify_go:
        mov rdi, r12;
        mov rsi, r13;
        mov rdx, r14;
        mov rcx, r15;
        mov r8,  rbx;
        call blob_load_verify_with_hash;

        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }
}
